<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Mankot State</h1><p class="st-meta">1400 CE – 1820 CE (approx.) · capital Mankot</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Rajput hill state between Jammu and Basohli, remembered for the Mankot school of Pahari painting.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Mankot, also called Ramkot, was ruled by the Mankotia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a>; tradition dates the state to around 1400, but its early history is poorly recorded.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Mankot court produced a distinctive school of Pahari <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-miniature">miniature painting</a> in the late 17th and 18th centuries, related to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/basohli-state">Basohli</a> style and known for bold portraits of local rulers. Politically it was a minor state in the shadow of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Mankot was absorbed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dogras">Dogra</a> rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> around 1820 and became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jammu-and-kashmir-princely-state">Jammu and Kashmir (princely state)</a> in 1846. The area, around Ramkot, is now in Jammu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="princely state, 1400 CE – 1820 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">princely state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Indian princely states</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:87.56%;width:8.36%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1400 CE – 1820 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mankot</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dogri</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ramkot, Mankot</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1610&amp;focus=mankot-state" aria-label="Mankot State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6756 1088 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mankot State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7076 1344L6918 1285"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6918 1285h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7076 1344h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7076 1344h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mankot. <a href="/atlas?year=1610&amp;focus=mankot-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

